lessons.1.10:
# Heap / Priority Queue

A heap gives you O(log n) insert and O(1) peek/remove of the min (or max) element — exactly what you need whenever a problem asks for "the k-th something" or "keep processing the smallest/largest remaining item."

## Min-heap vs. max-heap

A min-heap always keeps the smallest element at the top; a max-heap keeps the largest. JavaScript has no built-in heap, so in interviews you either implement a small binary heap or simulate one — but the concept transfers regardless of language:

```js
// Conceptual shape of a min-heap's core operations
class MinHeap {
  constructor() { this.data = []; }
  size() { return this.data.length; }
  peek() { return this.data[0]; }

  push(val) {
    this.data.push(val);
    this.#bubbleUp(this.data.length - 1);
  }

  pop() {
    const top = this.data[0];
    const last = this.data.pop();
    if (this.data.length) { this.data[0] = last; this.#bubbleDown(0); }
    return top;
  }

  #bubbleUp(i) {
    while (i > 0) {
      const parent = (i - 1) >> 1;
      if (this.data[parent] <= this.data[i]) break;
      [this.data[parent], this.data[i]] = [this.data[i], this.data[parent]];
      i = parent;
    }
  }

  #bubbleDown(i) {
    const n = this.data.length;
    while (true) {
      let smallest = i;
      const l = 2 * i + 1, r = 2 * i + 2;
      if (l < n && this.data[l] < this.data[smallest]) smallest = l;
      if (r < n && this.data[r] < this.data[smallest]) smallest = r;
      if (smallest === i) break;
      [this.data[i], this.data[smallest]] = [this.data[smallest], this.data[i]];
      i = smallest;
    }
  }
}
```

You won't usually need to write this from scratch (most languages have a built-in priority queue), but understanding `bubbleUp`/`bubbleDown` is why insert/remove are O(log n): you only ever walk up or down one path in a balanced binary tree stored as an array.

## K-th largest / smallest

Keep a heap of size `k`: for k-th largest, use a **min-heap** of size k — push each new element, and pop whenever the heap exceeds size k. Whatever survives on top after processing everything is the k-th largest. This is O(n log k), much better than sorting the whole array (O(n log n)) when k is small.

## Merge k sorted lists

Put the head of each list into a min-heap keyed by value. Repeatedly pop the smallest, append it to the result, and push that node's successor (if any) back into the heap. This processes all `n` total elements with O(log k) work each, for O(n log k) overall — far better than merging lists two at a time.

## Recognizing the pattern

"Kth largest/smallest," "top k frequent," "merge k sorted structures," or "continuously retrieve the min/max while the data keeps changing" are the signal phrases for a heap. If sorting the whole input would work but feels wasteful because you only need a few extreme values, a heap is the fix.

lessons.1.11:
# Backtracking

Backtracking is systematic trial-and-error: build a candidate solution incrementally, and the moment it can't possibly succeed, undo the last choice and try the next one. It's how you enumerate **all** valid configurations — subsets, permutations, combinations, or board arrangements — without missing any or duplicating work.

## The template

Every backtracking solution follows the same shape:

```js
function backtrack(path, choices) {
  if (isComplete(path)) {
    results.push([...path]); // record a copy, not the mutable reference
    return;
  }
  for (const choice of choices) {
    if (!isValid(choice, path)) continue;
    path.push(choice);        // choose
    backtrack(path, remainingChoices(choices, choice));
    path.pop();                // un-choose — this is the "backtrack" step
  }
}
```

The `path.pop()` after the recursive call is the entire idea: you're reusing the same array/state across all branches instead of allocating new copies, which keeps space usage down to the depth of recursion.

## Subsets

Every element is either "in" or "out" — this naturally forms a binary decision tree of depth `n`:

```js
function subsets(nums) {
  const results = [];
  function go(start, path) {
    results.push([...path]);
    for (let i = start; i < nums.length; i++) {
      path.push(nums[i]);
      go(i + 1, path); // i + 1, not start + 1 — never reuse the same index
      path.pop();
    }
  }
  go(0, []);
  return results;
}
```

## Permutations

Unlike subsets, order matters and every element must be used — track which indices are already placed:

```js
function permute(nums) {
  const results = [];
  const used = new Array(nums.length).fill(false);
  function go(path) {
    if (path.length === nums.length) { results.push([...path]); return; }
    for (let i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
      if (used[i]) continue;
      used[i] = true;
      path.push(nums[i]);
      go(path);
      path.pop();
      used[i] = false;
    }
  }
  go([]);
  return results;
}
```

## Combinations & constraint satisfaction (N-Queens style)

Combinations are subsets of a fixed size — same skeleton as `subsets`, with a length check added to the base case. N-Queens-style problems add a **validity check** before each choice (does placing a queen here conflict with any queen already placed?) — this is where "pruning" pays off: reject a bad choice as early as possible instead of building out the whole invalid branch first.

## Recognizing the pattern

Phrases like "all possible," "every combination," "every arrangement," or "find all ways to..." are the giveaway. If the problem wants *one* optimal answer rather than *all* answers, you likely want dynamic programming or greedy instead — backtracking's cost is proportional to the number of valid (and attempted-but-rejected) configurations, which is fine for enumeration but often too slow for pure optimization.

lessons.1.12:
# Intervals

Interval problems ("merge overlapping meetings," "can you attend all meetings," "insert a new interval") almost always start with the same move: **sort by start time**. Once sorted, overlap detection becomes a simple linear scan.

## Merge intervals

```js
function mergeIntervals(intervals) {
  if (!intervals.length) return [];
  intervals.sort((a, b) => a[0] - b[0]);
  const merged = [intervals[0]];
  for (let i = 1; i < intervals.length; i++) {
    const last = merged[merged.length - 1];
    const [start, end] = intervals[i];
    if (start <= last[1]) {
      last[1] = Math.max(last[1], end); // overlaps — extend
    } else {
      merged.push([start, end]); // no overlap — new group
    }
  }
  return merged;
}
```

The overlap check `start <= last[1]` is the whole trick: because the array is sorted by start time, you only ever need to compare against the **most recently merged** interval, never the whole history.

## Interval scheduling (maximize non-overlapping intervals)

This is a greedy problem in disguise: sort by **end time** instead of start time, and greedily take an interval whenever it doesn't overlap the last one you took. Sorting by end time (not start time) is what makes the greedy choice provably optimal — it always leaves the most room for future intervals.

```js
function maxNonOverlapping(intervals) {
  intervals.sort((a, b) => a[1] - b[1]);
  let count = 0, lastEnd = -Infinity;
  for (const [start, end] of intervals) {
    if (start >= lastEnd) { count++; lastEnd = end; }
  }
  return count;
}
```

## Overlap detection

Two intervals `[a, b]` and `[c, d]` overlap exactly when `a <= d && c <= b`. This single condition underlies "meeting rooms," "insert interval," and "employee free time" problems — the variation is usually in what you do once you know two intervals overlap (merge them, count a conflict, or find the gap between them).

## Recognizing the pattern

Any problem describing ranges, meetings, or time windows — and asking you to merge, count conflicts, or schedule optimally — wants: sort first (by start or end depending on the goal), then a single linear pass comparing each interval only to the most recent one you've committed to.

lessons.1.13:
# Graphs

A graph is just nodes and edges — but a huge share of real problems (grids, dependency chains, social networks, word ladders) are graphs wearing a disguise. Recognizing "this is secretly a graph" is half the battle.

## BFS vs. DFS traversal

Both visit every reachable node exactly once (with a `visited` set), but explore in different orders:

```js
function bfs(graph, start) {
  const visited = new Set([start]);
  const queue = [start];
  const order = [];
  while (queue.length) {
    const node = queue.shift();
    order.push(node);
    for (const neighbor of graph[node]) {
      if (!visited.has(neighbor)) {
        visited.add(neighbor);
        queue.push(neighbor);
      }
    }
  }
  return order;
}

function dfs(graph, node, visited = new Set()) {
  visited.add(node);
  const order = [node];
  for (const neighbor of graph[node]) {
    if (!visited.has(neighbor)) order.push(...dfs(graph, neighbor, visited));
  }
  return order;
}
```

**Use BFS** when you need the **shortest path in an unweighted graph** — BFS explores level by level, so the first time you reach a node is guaranteed via the fewest edges. **Use DFS** when you just need to visit everything, detect cycles, or explore as deep as possible before backtracking (connected components, topological ordering groundwork).

## Connected components

Run BFS or DFS from every unvisited node; each run discovers one full component. Count how many times you had to start a fresh traversal — that's the number of components. This is the base pattern behind "number of islands," "number of provinces," and friend-circle problems.

## Cycle detection

- **Undirected graph:** during DFS, if you reach a node that's visited and isn't the immediate parent you came from, there's a cycle.
- **Directed graph:** track nodes currently "in progress" (on the current DFS call stack) separately from "fully finished." Reaching a node that's still in-progress means a cycle; reaching one that's finished is fine.

## Matrix-as-graph problems

A 2D grid is a graph where each cell is a node connected to its (usually 4) neighbors. "Number of islands," "rotting oranges," and "shortest path in a grid with obstacles" are all standard BFS/DFS, just with neighbors computed as `(r±1, c)` and `(r, c±1)` instead of an adjacency list.

```js
function numIslands(grid) {
  const rows = grid.length, cols = grid[0].length;
  let count = 0;
  function sink(r, c) {
    if (r < 0 || c < 0 || r >= rows || c >= cols || grid[r][c] !== "1") return;
    grid[r][c] = "0"; // mark visited in place
    sink(r + 1, c); sink(r - 1, c); sink(r, c + 1); sink(r, c - 1);
  }
  for (let r = 0; r < rows; r++) {
    for (let c = 0; c < cols; c++) {
      if (grid[r][c] === "1") { count++; sink(r, c); }
    }
  }
  return count;
}
```

## Recognizing the pattern

Anything with "connections," "paths between," "islands," "shortest hops," or a dependency-like relationship is a graph problem. Grids are graphs. Rooms with doors are graphs. Word ladders (one word transforms to another by changing one letter) are graphs where words are nodes and an edge exists between words one letter apart.

lessons.1.14:
# Advanced Graphs

This unit covers the four "named algorithms" that show up once plain BFS/DFS isn't enough — when edges have weights, or you need global structure across the whole graph rather than just reachability.

## Union-Find (Disjoint Set Union)

Union-Find answers "are these two nodes in the same group?" and "merge these two groups" in **almost O(1)** time each (technically O(α(n)), the inverse Ackermann function — effectively constant for any input size you'll ever see):

```js
class UnionFind {
  constructor(n) {
    this.parent = Array.from({ length: n }, (_, i) => i);
    this.rank = new Array(n).fill(0);
  }
  find(x) {
    if (this.parent[x] !== x) this.parent[x] = this.find(this.parent[x]); // path compression
    return this.parent[x];
  }
  union(a, b) {
    const rootA = this.find(a), rootB = this.find(b);
    if (rootA === rootB) return false; // already connected — this edge would form a cycle
    if (this.rank[rootA] < this.rank[rootB]) [rootA, rootB] = [rootB, rootA];
    this.parent[rootB] = rootA;
    if (this.rank[rootA] === this.rank[rootB]) this.rank[rootA]++;
    return true;
  }
}
```

Used for: detecting cycles while building a graph edge-by-edge, counting connected components, and as a building block inside Kruskal's algorithm below.

## Dijkstra's shortest path

Finds shortest paths from one source to all nodes in a graph with **non-negative** edge weights, using a min-heap to always expand the currently-closest unvisited node next:

```js
function dijkstra(graph, source) {
  const dist = new Map([[source, 0]]);
  const heap = [[0, source]]; // [distance, node] — conceptually a min-heap by distance
  const visited = new Set();
  while (heap.length) {
    heap.sort((a, b) => a[0] - b[0]); // stand-in for a real priority queue
    const [d, node] = heap.shift();
    if (visited.has(node)) continue;
    visited.add(node);
    for (const [neighbor, weight] of graph[node] ?? []) {
      const newDist = d + weight;
      if (newDist < (dist.get(neighbor) ?? Infinity)) {
        dist.set(neighbor, newDist);
        heap.push([newDist, neighbor]);
      }
    }
  }
  return dist;
}
```

This is BFS's weighted cousin: BFS assumes every edge costs 1, so a plain queue works; Dijkstra needs a priority queue because the "closest" unvisited node isn't necessarily the one discovered earliest.

## Minimum Spanning Tree — Prim's and Kruskal's

An MST connects all nodes with the minimum possible total edge weight, no cycles. **Kruskal's** sorts all edges by weight and greedily adds each one unless it would form a cycle (checked via Union-Find). **Prim's** grows a single tree outward, always adding the cheapest edge that connects a new node (a min-heap, similar to Dijkstra's shape). Kruskal's is usually easier to reason about and implement in an interview.

## Topological sort

Only defined for **directed acyclic graphs (DAGs)** — orders nodes so every edge points from earlier to later in the ordering. Used for task scheduling with dependencies ("course prerequisites"). Kahn's algorithm: repeatedly remove nodes with in-degree 0 (no remaining dependencies), appending them to the order and decrementing their neighbors' in-degrees.

## Recognizing the pattern

Weighted "shortest path" → Dijkstra. "Connect everything as cheaply as possible" → MST (Kruskal's/Prim's). "Order these tasks given dependencies" → topological sort. "Will adding this edge create a cycle?" or "how many groups after all these merges?" → Union-Find.

lessons.1.15:
# Greedy

A greedy algorithm makes the locally best choice at every step and never reconsiders it — no backtracking, no exploring alternatives. The hard part isn't writing greedy code (it's usually short); it's **proving the greedy choice is actually safe**, because greedy is wrong far more often than it's right.

## Activity selection

Given a set of activities with start/end times, select the maximum number that don't overlap. The greedy insight: always pick the activity that **finishes earliest** among the remaining valid options — this leaves the most possible room for everything after it.

```js
function maxActivities(activities) {
  activities.sort((a, b) => a.end - b.end);
  let count = 0, lastEnd = -Infinity;
  for (const { start, end } of activities) {
    if (start >= lastEnd) { count++; lastEnd = end; }
  }
  return count;
}
```

This is the same shape as the interval-scheduling example in the Intervals unit — it's the textbook proof case for why "sort by end time" is provably optimal here.

## The exchange argument

This is *how* you prove a greedy strategy correct, and it's worth understanding even at a conceptual level for interviews: assume there's some optimal solution that does **not** follow the greedy rule at some step. Show that you can swap ("exchange") that step for the greedy choice without making the solution any worse. If that's always true, greedy is at least as good as optimal — so greedy IS optimal.

For activity selection: suppose an optimal solution's first pick isn't the earliest-finishing activity. Swap it for the earliest-finishing one — since it finishes no later, it can't block any activity the original first pick didn't already block. The solution stays valid and no shorter. Hence picking earliest-finish-first is safe.

## When greedy fails

Greedy does **not** work for 0/1 knapsack (picking the best value/weight ratio item first can lock you out of a better combination later) — that's exactly why 0/1 knapsack needs dynamic programming instead. The difference: knapsack's choices interact (taking one item affects what fits later in a way that isn't monotonic), while activity selection's choices don't (an earlier-finishing activity never disadvantages you).

## Recognizing when greedy is safe

Ask: "If I make the locally best choice now, could it ever come back to hurt me later?" If you can construct even one counterexample, greedy is wrong and you need DP or backtracking instead. If the problem has a clean sorting order and each choice provably doesn't foreclose better future choices (classic tell: interval/scheduling problems), greedy is usually the intended — and much faster — solution.

lessons.1.16:
# 1-D Dynamic Programming

DP is for problems with **overlapping subproblems** and **optimal substructure** — the answer to a bigger problem is built from answers to smaller versions of the same problem, and those smaller versions get recomputed many times if you don't cache them.

## The Fibonacci lens

Naive recursive Fibonacci is O(2ⁿ) because `fib(5)` calls `fib(4)` and `fib(3)`, and `fib(4)` *also* calls `fib(3)` — the same subproblem gets solved repeatedly:

```js
// Memoization (top-down): cache results the first time you compute them
function fib(n, memo = new Map()) {
  if (n <= 1) return n;
  if (memo.has(n)) return memo.get(n);
  const result = fib(n - 1, memo) + fib(n - 2, memo);
  memo.set(n, result);
  return result;
}

// Tabulation (bottom-up): build the answer iteratively, smallest to largest
function fibTab(n) {
  if (n <= 1) return n;
  let prev2 = 0, prev1 = 1;
  for (let i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
    [prev2, prev1] = [prev1, prev1 + prev2];
  }
  return prev1;
}
```

Both are O(n) instead of O(2ⁿ). **Memoization** is usually easier to derive from the recursive definition; **tabulation** is usually more space-efficient (notice `fibTab` uses O(1) space, while memoized recursion uses O(n) call-stack space).

## House robber

You can't rob two adjacent houses. At each house, you either skip it (keep the best total so far) or rob it (best total from two houses back, plus this house's value):

```js
function rob(nums) {
  let prevNoRob = 0, prevRob = 0;
  for (const n of nums) {
    const newRob = prevNoRob + n;
    prevNoRob = Math.max(prevNoRob, prevRob);
    prevRob = newRob;
  }
  return Math.max(prevNoRob, prevRob);
}
```

## Climbing stairs

Number of ways to reach step `n`, taking 1 or 2 steps at a time — literally Fibonacci with different base cases: `ways(n) = ways(n-1) + ways(n-2)`, because your last move was either a 1-step from `n-1` or a 2-step from `n-2`.

## Coin change

Minimum coins to make amount `n`. For each amount from 1 up to `n`, try every coin denomination and take the best result from `amount - coin`, plus one:

```js
function coinChange(coins, amount) {
  const dp = new Array(amount + 1).fill(Infinity);
  dp[0] = 0;
  for (let a = 1; a <= amount; a++) {
    for (const coin of coins) {
      if (coin <= a) dp[a] = Math.min(dp[a], dp[a - coin] + 1);
    }
  }
  return dp[amount] === Infinity ? -1 : dp[amount];
}
```

## How to approach any new 1-D DP problem

1. Define what `dp[i]` **means** in words first (e.g. "max money robbable from houses 0..i"). This is the step people skip and then get stuck.
2. Write the recurrence: how does `dp[i]` relate to `dp[i-1]`, `dp[i-2]`, etc.?
3. Identify base cases.
4. Decide: memoized recursion (easier to write, matches the recurrence directly) or tabulation (better space, avoids stack overflow on large `n`).

## Recognizing the pattern

"Number of ways to...", "minimum/maximum ... given you can't do X and Y together", or any problem where a brute-force recursive solution visibly recomputes the same smaller inputs — that's 1-D DP.

lessons.1.17:
# 2-D Dynamic Programming

Same core idea as 1-D DP, except state now depends on **two** changing quantities — two positions in a grid, or a position in each of two strings/sequences. The `dp` structure becomes a 2D table instead of a 1D array.

## Grid paths

Counting paths from top-left to bottom-right of a grid, moving only right or down: each cell's answer is the sum of the cell above and the cell to the left, since those are the only two ways to arrive.

```js
function uniquePaths(m, n) {
  const dp = Array.from({ length: m }, () => new Array(n).fill(1));
  for (let r = 1; r < m; r++) {
    for (let c = 1; c < n; c++) {
      dp[r][c] = dp[r - 1][c] + dp[r][c - 1];
    }
  }
  return dp[m - 1][n - 1];
}
```

The first row and column are all 1s (only one way to reach them: keep going straight) — that's the base case.

## Longest common subsequence (LCS)

`dp[i][j]` = length of the LCS of `text1[0..i)` and `text2[0..j)`. If the current characters match, extend the diagonal; otherwise take the best of dropping one character from either string:

```js
function longestCommonSubsequence(text1, text2) {
  const m = text1.length, n = text2.length;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: m + 1 }, () => new Array(n + 1).fill(0));
  for (let i = 1; i <= m; i++) {
    for (let j = 1; j <= n; j++) {
      dp[i][j] = text1[i - 1] === text2[j - 1]
        ? dp[i - 1][j - 1] + 1
        : Math.max(dp[i - 1][j], dp[i][j - 1]);
    }
  }
  return dp[m][n];
}
```

## Edit distance

Minimum insertions/deletions/substitutions to turn one string into another — same table shape as LCS, but every mismatch costs 1 and you take the min of three neighbors (insert, delete, substitute) instead of two:

```js
function editDistance(word1, word2) {
  const m = word1.length, n = word2.length;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: m + 1 }, () => new Array(n + 1).fill(0));
  for (let i = 0; i <= m; i++) dp[i][0] = i;
  for (let j = 0; j <= n; j++) dp[0][j] = j;
  for (let i = 1; i <= m; i++) {
    for (let j = 1; j <= n; j++) {
      dp[i][j] = word1[i - 1] === word2[j - 1]
        ? dp[i - 1][j - 1]
        : 1 + Math.min(dp[i - 1][j - 1], dp[i - 1][j], dp[i][j - 1]);
    }
  }
  return dp[m][n];
}
```

## 0/1 Knapsack

Maximize value within a weight limit, each item usable at most once. `dp[i][w]` = best value using the first `i` items with capacity `w`. For each item, either skip it (`dp[i-1][w]`) or take it if it fits (`dp[i-1][w-weight] + value`):

```js
function knapsack(weights, values, capacity) {
  const n = weights.length;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => new Array(capacity + 1).fill(0));
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let w = 0; w <= capacity; w++) {
      dp[i][w] = dp[i - 1][w]; // skip item i
      if (weights[i - 1] <= w) {
        dp[i][w] = Math.max(dp[i][w], dp[i - 1][w - weights[i - 1]] + values[i - 1]);
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[n][capacity];
}
```

This is exactly the problem where greedy (sort by value/weight ratio) fails — see the Greedy unit for why.

## Recognizing the pattern

Two strings being compared → LCS/edit-distance shape. A grid with movement constraints → grid-path shape. "Choose a subset under a capacity constraint, each item used once" → knapsack shape. If your state needs two indices to describe "where you are" in the problem, you're in 2-D DP territory.

lessons.1.18:
# Bit Manipulation & Math

This unit is a grab-bag of tricks that show up as one-off "gotcha" problems, plus the number-theory basics you need to reason about arithmetic problems correctly.

## XOR tricks

XOR (`^`) has three properties that make it uniquely useful: `a ^ a = 0`, `a ^ 0 = a`, and it's commutative/associative (order doesn't matter). That's the entire explanation for the classic "find the single number in an array where every other number appears twice" — XOR everything together and every pair cancels out, leaving only the unpaired value:

```js
function singleNumber(nums) {
  return nums.reduce((acc, n) => acc ^ n, 0);
}
```

XOR is also how you swap two variables without a temp variable (`a ^= b; b ^= a; a ^= b;`), though in practice destructuring (`[a, b] = [b, a]`) is clearer in JS — the trick is worth recognizing when you see it in older code or C-style languages.

## Bitmasking

Represent a **set of up to ~30 boolean flags** as a single integer, where bit `i` being 1 means "item i is included." This turns subset enumeration into iterating integers from `0` to `2ⁿ - 1`:

```js
function allSubsets(nums) {
  const n = nums.length;
  const results = [];
  for (let mask = 0; mask < (1 << n); mask++) {
    const subset = [];
    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
      if (mask & (1 << i)) subset.push(nums[i]);
    }
    results.push(subset);
  }
  return results;
}
```

Bitmasking also underlies "DP with state = which items have been used" problems (traveling salesman-style DP), where the mask **is** the DP state.

## Useful bit operations to have memorized

- `n & (n - 1)` clears the lowest set bit — used to count set bits (Brian Kernighan's algorithm) or check if `n` is a power of two (`n & (n-1) === 0`).
- `n & -n` isolates the lowest set bit.
- `n << k` / `n >> k` multiply/divide by `2^k`.

## GCD / LCM

Euclid's algorithm computes GCD in O(log(min(a,b))) — much faster than checking every number up to `min(a,b)`:

```js
function gcd(a, b) {
  return b === 0 ? a : gcd(b, a % b);
}
function lcm(a, b) {
  return (a * b) / gcd(a, b);
}
```

## Modular arithmetic

When a problem says "return the answer modulo 10⁹+7," it's a signal the true answer is enormous (common in combinatorics/DP counting problems) and would overflow fixed-size integers in other languages. Key rules: `(a + b) % m = ((a % m) + (b % m)) % m`, and similarly for multiplication — but **not** for division, which needs modular inverses (rarely required at this level; usually just apply mod after every addition/multiplication step to keep numbers small).

## Recognizing the pattern

"Find the one element that's different/unpaired" → XOR. "Track which of up to ~20-30 items are used" → bitmask. Any problem explicitly mentioning "modulo 10^9 + 7" → apply mod after every arithmetic operation, not just at the end.

lessons.1.2:
# Arrays & Hashing

This is the highest-frequency pattern family in interviews — a huge fraction of "easy" and "medium" problems reduce to "can you avoid a nested loop by remembering what you've already seen." That's what hashmaps and hashsets buy you.

## The core trick: trade memory for time

A brute-force approach to "have I seen this before?" or "does a complement exist?" is usually a nested loop — O(n²). A hashmap turns "have I seen X?" into an O(1) lookup, collapsing the whole thing to O(n) at the cost of O(n) extra space. Recognizing this trade is the single biggest unlock in this unit.

## Two-Sum: the canonical example

```js
function twoSum(nums, target) {
  const seen = new Map(); // value -> index
  for (let i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
    const complement = target - nums[i];
    if (seen.has(complement)) return [seen.get(complement), i];
    seen.set(nums[i], i);
  }
  return [];
}
```

Notice the map is built **as you iterate**, not upfront — this lets you find a pair in a single pass instead of two.

## Frequency counting

Any problem involving "most common," "anagram," or "count occurrences" wants a frequency map:

```js
function isAnagram(a, b) {
  if (a.length !== b.length) return false;
  const freq = {};
  for (const ch of a) freq[ch] = (freq[ch] ?? 0) + 1;
  for (const ch of b) {
    if (!freq[ch]) return false;
    freq[ch]--;
  }
  return true;
}
```

## Prefix sums

When a problem asks about a **range** ("sum of subarray from i to j", "does a subarray sum to k?"), precompute running totals so any range query becomes O(1):

```js
function subarraySumEqualsK(nums, k) {
  const prefixCount = new Map([[0, 1]]);
  let sum = 0, count = 0;
  for (const n of nums) {
    sum += n;
    count += prefixCount.get(sum - k) ?? 0;
    prefixCount.set(sum, (prefixCount.get(sum) ?? 0) + 1);
  }
  return count;
}
```

The trick: if `sum - k` has appeared before as a prefix sum, everything between that point and now sums to exactly `k`.

## Grouping / categorization

Group items by a computed key (sorted string for anagram groups, first letter, remainder mod k) using a hashmap of arrays:

```js
function groupAnagrams(strs) {
  const groups = new Map();
  for (const s of strs) {
    const key = [...s].sort().join("");
    if (!groups.has(key)) groups.set(key, []);
    groups.get(key).push(s);
  }
  return [...groups.values()];
}
```

## Recognizing the pattern in the wild

Ask yourself: "Do I need to remember something about elements I've already visited?" If yes — a count, a previous value, an index, a running sum — reach for a hashmap before reaching for a nested loop. This one question resolves most of Weeks 1–2 of your DSA schedule.

lessons.1.3:
# Two Pointers

Two pointers replaces a nested loop with two indices that move through the array in a coordinated way — usually dropping an O(n²) brute force to O(n).

## Opposite-direction pointers

Used when the array is **sorted** and you're looking for a pair or checking symmetry. Start one pointer at each end and move them toward each other based on a comparison.

```js
function twoSumSorted(nums, target) {
  let left = 0, right = nums.length - 1;
  while (left < right) {
    const sum = nums[left] + nums[right];
    if (sum === target) return [left, right];
    if (sum < target) left++;   // need a bigger sum
    else right--;                // need a smaller sum
  }
  return [];
}
```

The classic **container with most water** and **valid palindrome** problems use this exact shape.

## Same-direction pointers (fast/slow within an array)

One pointer marks "where the next valid element should go," the other scans ahead. Used for in-place removal/dedup:

```js
function removeDuplicates(nums) {
  let slow = 0;
  for (let fast = 1; fast < nums.length; fast++) {
    if (nums[fast] !== nums[slow]) {
      slow++;
      nums[slow] = nums[fast];
    }
  }
  return slow + 1; // new length
}
```

## Sorted-array techniques

Sorting first (O(n log n)) often unlocks a two-pointer O(n) pass afterward — this combo shows up constantly for "3Sum," "merge two sorted arrays," and "closest pair" problems. The mental model: sorting gives you *monotonic* behavior, which is exactly what lets a pointer move only in one direction and never need to backtrack.

## Recognizing when to use it

- The array is sorted, or you can sort it without breaking the problem.
- You're looking for a pair/triplet satisfying a sum or difference condition.
- You need to partition or compact an array in place without extra space.

If you catch yourself writing `for i { for j { ... } }` over a sorted array, stop — it's very likely a two-pointer problem in disguise.

lessons.1.4:
# Sliding Window

Sliding window is two-pointers' sibling, specialized for **contiguous subarray/substring** problems — "longest," "shortest," or "count of" a substring/subarray satisfying some condition.

## Fixed-size window

When the window size `k` is given directly, slide it across the array, adding the new element and removing the old one instead of recomputing from scratch:

```js
function maxSumSubarray(nums, k) {
  let windowSum = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < k; i++) windowSum += nums[i];
  let best = windowSum;
  for (let i = k; i < nums.length; i++) {
    windowSum += nums[i] - nums[i - k]; // add new, drop oldest
    best = Math.max(best, windowSum);
  }
  return best;
}
```

This turns an O(n·k) brute force into O(n).

## Variable-size window

When the window size isn't fixed — "smallest subarray with sum ≥ target," "longest substring without repeating characters" — grow the right edge until the condition breaks (or is satisfied), then shrink the left edge to recover validity:

```js
function longestUniqueSubstring(s) {
  const seen = new Set();
  let left = 0, best = 0;
  for (let right = 0; right < s.length; right++) {
    while (seen.has(s[right])) {
      seen.delete(s[left]);
      left++;
    }
    seen.add(s[right]);
    best = Math.max(best, right - left + 1);
  }
  return best;
}
```

Notice `left` only ever moves forward — it never resets. That's what keeps this O(n) instead of O(n²): each character is added to the set once and removed once, across the whole run.

## Substring problems specifically

Problems like "minimum window substring" combine sliding window with a frequency map: expand until the window contains all required characters, then contract while it still does, tracking the smallest valid window seen.

## Recognizing the pattern

Ask: "Am I looking at every contiguous chunk of the array/string, and does a brute force re-scan each chunk from scratch?" If yes, sliding window almost certainly turns it linear. The giveaway phrases in problem statements are "subarray," "substring," "contiguous," and "window."

lessons.1.5:
# Stack

A stack (LIFO — last in, first out) is the right structure whenever a problem involves **nesting** or "the most recent unresolved thing matters most."

## Parentheses / bracket matching

The canonical use case: push opening brackets, and when you see a closing bracket, check it matches the top of the stack.

```js
function isValid(s) {
  const pairs = { ")": "(", "]": "[", "}": "{" };
  const stack = [];
  for (const ch of s) {
    if (!pairs[ch]) { stack.push(ch); continue; }
    if (stack.pop() !== pairs[ch]) return false;
  }
  return stack.length === 0;
}
```

## Monotonic stack

A monotonic stack keeps its elements in strictly increasing or decreasing order by popping anything that violates the order before pushing the new element. This is the pattern behind "next greater element," "daily temperatures," and "largest rectangle in histogram."

```js
function nextGreaterElement(nums) {
  const result = new Array(nums.length).fill(-1);
  const stack = []; // stores indices, values are decreasing top-to-bottom is wrong direction — see below
  for (let i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
    while (stack.length && nums[stack[stack.length - 1]] < nums[i]) {
      const idx = stack.pop();
      result[idx] = nums[i];
    }
    stack.push(i);
  }
  return result;
}
```

Walk through it: the stack holds indices whose "next greater element" hasn't been found yet. Every time a bigger value shows up, it resolves everyone smaller than it still sitting on the stack — each index is pushed once and popped once, so this is O(n) despite the nested-looking `while`.

## Why "next greater element" specifically matters

It's the template for a whole family: next smaller element, previous greater element, stock span, and histogram-area problems are all the same monotonic-stack skeleton with the comparison direction flipped.

## Recognizing the pattern

- "Valid parentheses / matching pairs" → plain stack.
- "Next/previous greater or smaller element" → monotonic stack.
- You're processing a sequence and need to look back at "the most recent unresolved item" → stack, not a hashmap or a second loop.

lessons.1.6:
# Binary Search

Binary search isn't just "find a value in a sorted array" — it's a general technique for cutting a search space in half whenever you can answer "is the target to the left or right of here?" in O(1).

## The classic form

```js
function binarySearch(nums, target) {
  let lo = 0, hi = nums.length - 1;
  while (lo <= hi) {
    const mid = lo + Math.floor((hi - lo) / 2); // avoids overflow, and avoids re-checking mid
    if (nums[mid] === target) return mid;
    if (nums[mid] < target) lo = mid + 1;
    else hi = mid - 1;
  }
  return -1;
}
```

Two things trip people up: using `mid = (lo + hi) / 2` (fine in JS, but the habit matters in languages with fixed-width integers), and getting the loop condition (`<=` vs `<`) wrong, which causes infinite loops or missed elements.

## "Search on the answer"

This is the pattern that turns binary search from a lookup trick into a genuine algorithm design tool. Instead of searching an array, you binary-search over the **space of possible answers** — useful whenever the problem has a monotonic "if X works, does X+1 also work?" property.

Example: "minimum days to make m bouquets" or "split array into k subarrays minimizing the largest sum" — you binary search over the candidate answer value, and for each candidate, run an O(n) feasibility check:

```js
function canSplit(nums, k, maxSum) {
  let pieces = 1, current = 0;
  for (const n of nums) {
    if (current + n > maxSum) { pieces++; current = n; }
    else current += n;
  }
  return pieces <= k;
}

function splitArray(nums, k) {
  let lo = Math.max(...nums), hi = nums.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0);
  while (lo < hi) {
    const mid = lo + Math.floor((hi - lo) / 2);
    if (canSplit(nums, k, mid)) hi = mid; else lo = mid + 1;
  }
  return lo;
}
```

## Rotated sorted arrays

A rotated sorted array (e.g. `[4,5,6,7,0,1,2]`) is still binary-searchable — at every midpoint, **one half is always properly sorted**. Check which half is sorted, then decide whether the target lies in that sorted half or the other one.

## Recognizing the pattern

- The array is sorted (or "sorted with a rotation").
- The problem asks for a minimum/maximum value satisfying a condition, and that condition is monotonic (once true, stays true as the candidate increases, or vice versa) — "search on the answer" applies even with no array in sight.

lessons.1.7:
# Linked List

Linked lists are where "draw it on paper first" pays off most — almost every bug in this unit comes from losing track of a pointer, not from misunderstanding the algorithm.

## Reversal

The core skill: walk the list once, re-pointing each node's `next` to the previous node instead of the next one.

```js
function reverseList(head) {
  let prev = null, curr = head;
  while (curr) {
    const next = curr.next; // save before overwriting
    curr.next = prev;
    prev = curr;
    curr = next;
  }
  return prev; // new head
}
```

## Fast-slow pointers (Floyd's cycle detection)

Move one pointer one step at a time, another two steps at a time. If there's a cycle, the fast pointer eventually laps the slow one and they meet — this is the standard way to detect a cycle in O(n) time and O(1) space, without a visited-set.

```js
function hasCycle(head) {
  let slow = head, fast = head;
  while (fast && fast.next) {
    slow = slow.next;
    fast = fast.next.next;
    if (slow === fast) return true;
  }
  return false;
}
```

The same technique finds the **middle of a linked list** in one pass: when `fast` reaches the end, `slow` is at the midpoint.

## Merge operations

Merging two sorted lists is a direct analog of the merge step in merge sort — compare heads, attach the smaller, advance that list:

```js
function mergeTwoLists(a, b) {
  const dummy = { next: null };
  let tail = dummy;
  while (a && b) {
    if (a.val <= b.val) { tail.next = a; a = a.next; }
    else { tail.next = b; b = b.next; }
    tail = tail.next;
  }
  tail.next = a ?? b;
  return dummy.next;
}
```

## The dummy-node technique

Whenever the **head itself might change** (removing the first node, merging lists, partitioning), create a placeholder `dummy` node pointing at the real head. Do all your work relative to `dummy`, and return `dummy.next` at the end. This removes an entire category of "special case for the first node" bugs.

## Recognizing the pattern

- Reversal, palindrome-checking, and "reorder list" problems → reversal + fast/slow.
- Cycle detection, middle-of-list, "nth node from the end" → fast/slow pointers.
- Any operation that might delete/replace the head node → dummy node, no exceptions.

lessons.1.8:
# Trees

Trees are recursive by nature, so the biggest unlock in this unit is trusting recursion: assume the function already works correctly on subtrees, and write the combining step.

## DFS traversals: preorder, inorder, postorder

```js
function preorder(node, out = []) {
  if (!node) return out;
  out.push(node.val);      // visit
  preorder(node.left, out);
  preorder(node.right, out);
  return out;
}
```

- **Preorder** (visit → left → right): good for copying/serializing a tree.
- **Inorder** (left → visit → right): visits a **BST** in sorted order — this is the single most useful fact in this unit.
- **Postorder** (left → right → visit): good when children must be fully processed before the parent (computing subtree sizes, deleting a tree, evaluating an expression tree).

## BFS / level order

Level order needs a queue, not recursion, since you're processing the tree breadth-first:

```js
function levelOrder(root) {
  if (!root) return [];
  const result = [];
  let queue = [root];
  while (queue.length) {
    const level = [];
    const next = [];
    for (const node of queue) {
      level.push(node.val);
      if (node.left) next.push(node.left);
      if (node.right) next.push(node.right);
    }
    result.push(level);
    queue = next;
  }
  return result;
}
```

## BST properties

In a binary search tree, every node's left subtree is entirely smaller and its right subtree entirely larger. This gives you O(log n) search/insert/delete on a **balanced** BST, and it means an inorder traversal always yields sorted output — a fact that instantly solves "validate BST" and "kth smallest element" problems.

## Tree construction / serialization

Building a tree from traversal arrays (e.g. preorder + inorder) relies on: the first element of preorder is always the root, and that value's position in inorder splits the array into left/right subtrees. Serialization (tree ↔ string) typically uses preorder with explicit null markers so it can be deserialized unambiguously.

## Recognizing the pattern

Default to recursion for anything phrased as "for each subtree..." Reach for BFS/queue specifically when the problem talks about **levels** ("level order," "right side view," "zigzag traversal," "minimum depth" via shortest path). Reach for BST properties whenever the input is explicitly a BST — most tree problems become much easier once you use the sorted-order guarantee instead of treating it like a generic binary tree.

lessons.1.9:
# Tries

A trie (prefix tree) is a tree where **each path from the root spells out a prefix**, and it exists to answer one question extremely fast: "does any word in my dictionary start with this prefix?"

## Structure

Each node holds a map of `character -> child node`, plus a flag for "a word ends here":

```js
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.children = new Map();
    this.isWord = false;
  }
}

class Trie {
  constructor() { this.root = new TrieNode(); }

  insert(word) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const ch of word) {
      if (!node.children.has(ch)) node.children.set(ch, new TrieNode());
      node = node.children.get(ch);
    }
    node.isWord = true;
  }

  search(word) {
    const node = this.#walk(word);
    return !!node && node.isWord;
  }

  startsWith(prefix) {
    return !!this.#walk(prefix);
  }

  #walk(str) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const ch of str) {
      if (!node.children.has(ch)) return null;
      node = node.children.get(ch);
    }
    return node;
  }
}
```

Both `insert` and `search`/`startsWith` are O(k) where `k` is the word/prefix length — completely independent of how many words are stored. That's the whole point: a hashset of strings can tell you "is this exact word present," but it can't answer "does any word start with this prefix" without scanning every entry.

## Prefix / autocomplete patterns

Autocomplete is: walk the trie to the end of the typed prefix, then DFS from that node collecting every complete word below it. Word search on a grid (finding trie-stored words embedded in a 2D board) combines a trie with DFS/backtracking — you walk the grid and the trie in lockstep, pruning as soon as the current path isn't a valid prefix in the trie.

## Recognizing the pattern

Any problem that repeatedly asks "does a prefix exist," "autocomplete," or "search a dictionary of words against a grid" wants a trie. If you find yourself scanning a whole word list per query, a trie is almost certainly the intended structure.

lessons.3.1:
# Unit Testing (Jest)

Testing is one of the cheapest gaps to close and one of the most commonly screened-for skills — "write clean, maintainable code and perform unit testing" shows up almost verbatim in real job postings.

## Test structure: describe / it

```js
describe("calculateTotal", () => {
  it("sums item prices", () => {
    expect(calculateTotal([{ price: 10 }, { price: 5 }])).toBe(15);
  });

  it("returns 0 for an empty cart", () => {
    expect(calculateTotal([])).toBe(0);
  });
});
```

`describe` groups related tests under a shared label; `it` (or `test`) declares a single expected behavior. Name each `it` block as a sentence describing behavior ("returns 0 for an empty cart"), not implementation ("loops through array") — the test name is documentation.

## Assertions

```js
expect(result).toBe(5);                 // strict equality (===)
expect(result).toEqual({ a: 1 });       // deep equality for objects/arrays
expect(fn).toThrow("Invalid input");    // asserts an error is thrown
expect(array).toContain(3);
expect(value).toBeNull();
```

`toBe` vs `toEqual` trips people up constantly: `toBe` compares references for objects (two separately-created identical objects are `toBe`-unequal), `toEqual` compares structural/deep equality. Use `toEqual` for objects and arrays.

## Mocking functions

Replace a real dependency (an API call, a database, `Date.now()`) with a fake you control, so the test is isolated and deterministic:

```js
const fetchUser = jest.fn().mockResolvedValue({ id: 1, name: "Prasanth" });

test("greets the fetched user", async () => {
  const user = await fetchUser();
  expect(fetchUser).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(1);
  expect(greet(user)).toBe("Hello, Prasanth");
});
```

Mocking is what keeps unit tests fast and reliable — a real network call would make the test slow, flaky, and dependent on an external service being up.

## beforeEach / afterEach

Run shared setup/teardown around every test in a block, so each test starts from a known clean state instead of leaking state between tests:

```js
let db;
beforeEach(() => { db = createInMemoryDb(); });
afterEach(() => { db.close(); });
```

## Code coverage

Coverage reports (`jest --coverage`) tell you what percentage of lines/branches your tests actually execute — useful as a **signal for gaps**, not a target to game. 100% coverage with weak assertions (`expect(result).toBeDefined()` everywhere) is worse than 70% coverage that actually verifies behavior. Use coverage to find code nobody is testing, not as the definition of "done."

## Practice tip

Retrofit tests onto one existing component you already built, rather than starting from a fresh toy example — testing real code (with its real edge cases and real dependencies to mock) is what actually builds the skill the roadmap's Month 1 project asks for.

lessons.3.2:
# Component/Integration Testing (React Testing Library)

RTL's entire philosophy is captured in one sentence from its docs: **"the more your tests resemble the way your software is used, the more confidence they can give you."** This unit is about testing behavior, not implementation.

## Behavior over implementation details

A brittle test reaches into component internals (state variables, class names, instance methods). A good RTL test interacts with the component the way a user would — clicking, typing, reading visible text — so refactors that don't change behavior don't break the test:

```jsx
// Brittle: breaks if you rename a CSS class or refactor internal state
expect(wrapper.find(".submit-btn").prop("disabled")).toBe(true);

// Resilient: only breaks if the actual user-visible behavior changes
expect(screen.getByRole("button", { name: /submit/i })).toBeDisabled();
```

## Querying by role/text, not CSS class

RTL's query priority (accessible-role queries first) exists on purpose — it doubles as an accessibility check, since a query like `getByRole("button", { name: "Submit" })` only works if the element is genuinely accessible (has the right role and an accessible name):

```jsx
screen.getByRole("textbox", { name: /email/i });
screen.getByText(/welcome back/i);
screen.getByLabelText(/password/i);
```

Reach for `getByTestId` only as a last resort when no accessible query fits — it's an escape hatch, not the default.

## Simulating user events

```jsx
import { render, screen } from "@testing-library/react";
import userEvent from "@testing-library/user-event";

test("submits the form with entered email", async () => {
  const user = userEvent.setup();
  render(<LoginForm onSubmit={handleSubmit} />);

  await user.type(screen.getByLabelText(/email/i), "test@example.com");
  await user.click(screen.getByRole("button", { name: /log in/i }));

  expect(handleSubmit).toHaveBeenCalledWith({ email: "test@example.com" });
});
```

`userEvent` (not the lower-level `fireEvent`) is preferred because it simulates the full sequence of real browser events (focus, keydown, keyup, input) a user's action would actually trigger.

## Async utilities

Anything involving a fetch call, a timeout, or a state update after a promise resolves needs `findBy*` (returns a promise, retries until found) or `waitFor`:

```jsx
test("shows fetched user name", async () => {
  render(<UserProfile userId={1} />);
  expect(await screen.findByText("Prasanth Kumar Jha")).toBeInTheDocument();
});
```

Using `getBy*` here would fail immediately, before the async data has arrived — `findBy*` polls until it appears or times out.

## Mocking API calls

Intercept network requests at the boundary (commonly with `msw` — Mock Service Worker) so components are tested with realistic request/response cycles without hitting a real backend:

```js
const server = setupServer(
  http.get("/api/user/:id", () => HttpResponse.json({ name: "Prasanth" }))
);
```

## Practice tip

Pick one component with actual user interaction (a form, a toggle, a list with filtering) and test it purely through what a user sees and does — no direct state or prop inspection. If you can't write the test without reaching into internals, that's often a sign the component's public interface needs rethinking, not that RTL is being difficult.
